UID:india-foundation-arts-research-programme-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: India Foundation for the Arts: Arts Research Programme (M
 ay 2026 Request for Proposals)
DESCRIPTION:IFA's Arts Research Programme funds research into forms of know
 ing and thinking rooted in creative\, scholarly or cultural practice. It e
 xplicitly welcomes experimental and self-reflexive methodologies\, practic
 e-based inquiry\, interdisciplinary dialogue\, community engagement\, tran
 slation studies and work on marginalised narratives\, with a shareable pub
 lic outcome expected. Open to artists\, scholars\, curators\, translators\
 , educators and practitioner-researchers. ELIGIBILITY IS RESTRICTED: India
 n nationals only\, and PIO and OCI card holders are explicitly excluded\; 
 any collaborators must also be Indian nationals. Applicants need a PAN car
 d and valid ID. PROCESS NOTE: this is a two-stage email application\, not 
 a web form. Applicants were expected to discuss the project concept with t
 he Programme Officer (harshita@indiaifa.org) by 11 July 2026\, with the fi
 nal proposal\, CV and budget breakdown emailed by 10 August 2026 at 23:59 
 IST\, in an email no larger than 25 MB. Proposals may be written in any In
 dian language\, including English.\n\nOrganisation: India Foundation for t
 he Arts (IFA)\nLocation: India\; project-based with no residency requireme
 nt. Open to Indian nationals only.\nAward: Up to INR 500\,000 (around USD 
 5\,700 / EUR 4\,900) per project. Honorarium is capped at 35 percent of th
 e budget. Institutional overhead\, building costs\, infrastructure and equ
 ipment purchases are excluded. No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 12 m
 onths\, maximum 18 months.\n\nMore: https://indiaifa.org/whats-new/ar-rfp-
 may2026.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://indiaifa.org/whats-new/ar-rfp-may2026.html?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:India\; project-based with no residency requirement. Open to India
 n nationals only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pom-fukuoka-2027-call-for-abstracts@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: POM Fukuoka 2027: Politics of the Machines\, Call for Abs
 tracts
DESCRIPTION:Call for abstracts for the POM (Politics of the Machines) confe
 rence in Fukuoka\, hosted on Kyushu University's Ohashi Campus\, organised
  around the theme of transduction. TRACKS: 01 The Landscape of Transductio
 n: Infrastructural Entanglements of Energy\, Data\, and More-than-Human Li
 fe\; 02 Parasitic Transductions Beyond the Human\; 03 Real Machines: Compu
 tation\, Subjectivity\, and the Politics of Reality-Production\; 04 Techno
 genetic Transductions across Fields of Experience: Affective Politics in/f
 or Times of Crisis\; 05 Transducing Disgust: Bodies\, Technologies\, and t
 he Synthetic Uncanny\; 06 Methoding Transduction: Sound\, Data\, Sonic Res
 earch\; 07 Metabolic Worldviews and Hybrid Intelligences\; 08 Fermentative
  Systems\; 09 Unhearing Bodies: Life as Transduction\; and 10\, an open tr
 ack for Speculations and Culturing Immature Ideas. FORMATS: academic paper
 s\, lecture-performances\, artistic interventions and workshops\, with pro
 posals for alternative formats also encouraged\; indicate the intended for
 mat in the submission. FACILITIES: beyond the Multi Purpose Building and D
 esign Library\, the campus offers a BioFoodLab (a Biolab and a Foodlab\, e
 ach holding roughly 20 people) and an Acoustic Research Center (recording 
 studio for up to 50\, anechoic chamber for up to 15\, echo chamber / rever
 beration room for up to 10\, and semi-anechoic chamber for up to 20)\, whi
 ch the organisers flag as relevant to tracks engaging sound\, food\, perce
 ption\, signal and technological mediation. SUBMIT: an abstract of up to 5
 00 words under one of the tracks\; submissions without a track will be ass
 igned to the most appropriate one. All submissions go through double-blind
  peer review\, and authors of accepted abstracts are invited to present. T
 he submission platform is described as opening soon. AFTER: accepted autho
 rs are invited to submit a full paper of up to 4\,000 words including refe
 rences\, with a formal call for papers to follow. COSTS: note that this is
  a self-funded opportunity. Presenting requires paid conference registrati
 on\, and POM cannot cover travel\, accommodation\, registration or other p
 articipation expenses. DEADLINE: 30 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: POM (Pol
 itics of the Machines) Conference\, hosted at Kyushu University Ohashi Cam
 pus\nLocation: Fukuoka\, Japan (Kyushu University Ohashi Campus\, in perso
 n)\nAward: Unpaid\, and self-funded. POM explicitly cannot provide financi
 al support for travel\, accommodation\, registration or other participatio
 n costs\, and authors of accepted abstracts must register for the conferen
 ce in order to present. No abstract submission fee.\nDuration: Conference 
 in 2027\; accepted authors are later invited to submit a full paper of up 
 to 4\,000 words\n\nMore: https://pom-conference.org/fukuoka-2027/?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pom-conference.org/fukuoka-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fukuoka\, Japan (Kyushu University Ohashi Campus\, in person)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:antikythera-agentworld-journal-cfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Antikythera Journal: Agentworld Special Issue\, Call for 
 Papers
DESCRIPTION:Antikythera\, a research programme on planetary computation and
  the evolution of intelligence\, invites contributions to Agentworld\, a s
 pecial issue of its peer-reviewed MIT Press journal launching Fall 2026. T
 HEME: Agentworld is a research initiative on the medium-term future of hyb
 rid human-AI societies\, framed not around tools for agentic task completi
 on but around what the brief calls 'the human perplexity of deep agentic d
 iffusion'. It treats human societies as the evolutionary niches into which
  new artificial species must adapt\, where the constraints of agentic inte
 gration are qualitative and open-ended (psychological\, philosophical\, li
 nguistic\, symbolic\, existential) rather than utility functions to be sol
 ved. The framing describes a 'parasociety' of densely reticulated social g
 raphs in which agent-to-agent communication constitutes most socially sign
 ificant interaction\, humans and agents anthropomorphise each other by inh
 eritance rather than necessity\, and the two overlapping societies act as 
 mutual evolutionary niches. The disciplinary posture proposed is 'a preemp
 tive anthropology of open-world centaur societies': building the vocabular
 y\, scenarios and conceptual armature for an emergent sociality while it i
 s still forming\, rather than describing it after the fact. WHAT THEY WANT
 : theoretical essays and technical papers that engage the Agentworld theme
 s directly and explain how the contribution adds to the special issue's co
 re questions. Philosophical\, theoretical\, technical\, design-led and pro
 duct-oriented research are all in scope. The call explicitly favours work 
 that exceeds conventional academic journal formats\, including contributio
 ns combining philosophical argument\, technical analysis\, design research
 \, diagrams\, software\, infrastructure proposals\, visual media or web-na
 tive formats. Contributors may optionally propose still images\, diagrams\
 , video\, web-based or interactive components\, and should say whether the
 y have the design\, development or production capacity to realise them\; t
 hese elements are not required. SUBMIT: an abstract or proposal of up to 5
 00 words\; author name(s)\, affiliation(s)\, contact details\, biography(i
 es) and CV(s)\; a designated lead author or contact person for collaborati
 ve submissions\; and a portfolio with at least one relevant work sample su
 ch as a preprint\, published article or paper. Full paper drafts are encou
 raged where available but not required at the initial stage. Submissions m
 ay be individual or collaborative. TIMING: the review window opened 25 Jun
 e 2026. NOTE: no closing date is published on the call page\; the deadline
  shown here is a placeholder set roughly a month out\, so check the site o
 r submit early.\n\nOrganisation: Antikythera (Antikythera: Journal for the
  Philosophy of Planetary Computation\, published with MIT Press)\nLocation
 : Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-forward)
 \nAward: Unpaid. No honorarium or fee is offered for accepted contribution
 s\; the return is peer-reviewed publication in the MIT Press-published Ant
 ikythera Journal. No submission fee.\nDuration: Single publication\; first
  selection of articles published Fall 2026\n\nMore: https://agentworld.ant
 ikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral#ca
 ll-for-papers
URL:https://agentworld.antikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral#call-for-papers
LOCATION:Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-fo
 rward)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lse-digipulse-research-officer-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: LSE Research Officer: DigiPulse\, Digital Futures for Chi
 ldren Centre
DESCRIPTION:DigiPulse is a three-year longitudinal\, international research
  project in LSE's Digital Futures for Children centre investigating the re
 al-time relationships between children's smartphone use and their wellbein
 g\, with data collected in the UK\, Brazil and Kenya. The project combines
  passive smartphone tracking\, Ecological Momentary Assessment (EMA) and l
 ongitudinal surveys with around 750 children and young people aged 10-17. 
 THE ROLE: the Research Officer plays a central part in project management\
 , development\, data collection and analysis\, with particular responsibil
 ity for developing and refining EMA instruments\, contributing to the rese
 arch literature and hypotheses\, supporting participatory and consultative
  work with children\, and conducting statistical analysis and producing fi
 ndings\, working closely with partners in the UK\, Brazil and Kenya. REQUI
 REMENTS: a completed PhD in a relevant discipline (e.g. media and communic
 ations\, psychology\, psychiatry\, sociology\, education\, or computationa
 l/data social science)\; experience developing surveys or questionnaire in
 struments\; experience designing or conducting longitudinal research studi
 es\; and experience with EMA\, experience-sampling or other intensive long
 itudinal data collection methods. APPLY via jobs.lse.ac.uk\; questions to 
 k.g.ringmar-sylwander@lse.ac.uk. Closing date 22 July 2026 (23:59 UK time)
 \; late applications are not accepted.\n\nOrganisation: London School of E
 conomics and Political Science (LSE)\, Department of Media and Communicati
 ons\nLocation: London\, UK (hybrid working)\nAward: GBP 43\,277 to 51\,714
  per year inclusive of London allowance\, with potential to progress to GB
 P 55\,497. Full-time (1.0 FTE)\, funded until December 2028. Benefits: occ
 upational pension scheme\, generous annual leave\, hybrid working\, traini
 ng and development. No application fee.\nDuration: Full-time fixed-term po
 st\, funded until December 2028\n\nMore: https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/
 W/2504/0/471333/15539/research-officer-digipulse-digital-futures-for-child
 ren-centre/Referral?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/2504/0/471333/15539/research-officer
 -digipulse-digital-futures-for-children-centre/Referral?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK (hybrid working)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:konior-research-assistant-ai-ukraine-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260723
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:Deadline: Paid Research Assistant: AI and the War in Ukraine
DESCRIPTION:Bogna Konior\, Assistant Professor of Media Theory at NYU Shang
 hai\, is hiring a paid research assistant for a project on Ukraine and art
 ificial intelligence. The work supports a forthcoming book with MIT Press\
 , plus an independent scenario she is writing on the future of AI with the
  war as a lens. WHAT THE WORK LOOKS LIKE: going month-by-month through new
 s archives (New York Times\, Reuters\, Financial Times\, and specialist ou
 tlets)\, annotating and cataloguing coverage of AI\, drones\, and autonomo
 us systems in the war\, plus other developments she will direct you to\; b
 uilding and maintaining a structured Notion database covering chronology\,
  companies\, industries\, and key actors\; and organizing and editing her 
 existing notes. WHO SHE IS LOOKING FOR: interest in and general knowledge 
 of AI\; prior research and annotation experience\, careful and detail-orie
 nted\, no sloppy citations\; comfortable with Notion and with AI tools\; U
 krainian language skill a strong plus (not required)\; familiarity with AI
 \, war\, tech\, and geopolitics news sources is great. Remote-friendly\, s
 tart ASAP. She will also consider people with no prior experience\, as wel
 l as current and previous students. TERMS: USD 18 per hour\, 10-15 hours p
 er week\, flexible schedule. TO APPLY: email bogna@nyu.edu with a short in
 troduction and background (link to website\, socials\, CV\, LinkedIn\, or 
 portfolio if you have one)\; where you are based and your weekly availabil
 ity\; and one recent AI-related story\, company\, or development you find 
 interesting and why (a vibe check). Bonus: a sample of past research or wr
 iting if you have it.\n\nOrganisation: Bogna Konior (NYU Shanghai)\nLocati
 on: Remote-friendly.\nAward: USD 18 per hour\; 10-15 hours per week\, flex
 ible schedule.\nDuration: Ongoing part-time research assistantship\; start
  as soon as possible.\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com/p/Dak-bwACIMt/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/Dak-bwACIMt/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote-friendly.
END:VEVENT
