SUMMARY:Deadline: Programme PAUSE: Emergency Hosting for Scientists and Art
 ists in Exile (autumn 2026 call)
DESCRIPTION:PAUSE is the French national programme for the emergency hostin
 g of scientists and artists in exile\, created in 2017 and run from the Co
 llege de France. It co-funds a stay at a French higher education or resear
 ch establishment or at a cultural institution so that a researcher\, docto
 ral candidate or artist at risk can keep working\, and it also supports th
 eir family. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be able to prove status as a doct
 oral student (with a thesis in progress interrupted by the emergency)\, re
 searcher\, teacher-researcher or artist-teacher in their country of origin
 \; must be in a crisis situation because of the security situation in that
  country or because of persecution or fear of persecution on grounds of et
 hnicity\, religion\, political convictions\, opinions or sexual orientatio
 n\, or the content of their research or teaching\; and must be forced into
  exile or have been in France for less than three years. HOST REQUIREMENT:
  a French host institution is mandatory and submits the application\; elig
 ible hosts are institutions under the French higher education and research
  ministry\, EESPIG private institutions\, and structures supported by the 
 Ministry of Culture. PAUSE publishes a list of at-risk candidates looking 
 for a host\, which institutions can use to find a match. The call opens at
  the end of August 2026 with a deadline of 24 September 2026.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Programme PAUSE\, hosted by the College de France\, with the French M
 inistries of Higher Education and Research and of Culture\nLocation: Franc
 e (hosting at a French higher education\, research or cultural institution
 )\nAward: PAUSE co-funds the hosting project (salary and support costs) to
 gether with the host institution\; amounts depend on the project and insti
 tution. No application fee.\nDuration: Hosting periods are typically 12 mo
 nths\, renewable subject to review\n\nMore: https://www.programmepause.fr/
 en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.programmepause.fr/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:France (hosting at a French higher education\, research or cultura
 l institution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:glina3-open-call-type-language-visual-systems-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: GLINA3: Open Call for a Collaborator Working with Type\, 
 Language and Visual Systems
DESCRIPTION:GLINA3 is an Amsterdam clay 3D printing and digital design stud
 io\, and it is looking for one collaborator to join a new research project
  at the intersection of typography\, language\, technology and material cu
 lture. They want an artist\, writer\, poet\, anthropologist or artistic re
 searcher whose existing practice already engages with type design\, typogr
 aphy\, writing systems\, visual language\, fonts and letterforms\, languag
 e technologies\, or the tools that shape how language is produced\, read o
 r translated. This is an unusually good fit for anyone working on the mach
 ine-readable side of language: the brief explicitly names language technol
 ogies and translation tooling alongside classical type design. ELIGIBILITY
 : individual practitioners with demonstrable work in one or more of the li
 sted areas. HOW TO APPLY: the studio asks for relevant work\, research\, p
 ublications or projects connected to type\, fonts\, writing systems\, visu
 al language or the tools that shape them\, and nothing else. Because the d
 eadline is tight they say Instagram DM is the fastest route\; you can also
  email a short introduction and one relevant project to office@glina3.nl. 
 NOTE: the fee is confirmed only if the project's own funding comes through
 \, so clarify terms before committing time.\n\nOrganisation: GLINA3\nLocat
 ion: Amsterdam\, Netherlands (studio is Amsterdam-based\; collaboration te
 rms not specified)\nAward: Paid collaboration. The studio states the fee w
 ill be confirmed upon successful funding\, so the amount is contingent and
  not published up front. No application fee.\nDuration: New collaborative 
 research project\, length not specified\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com
 /p/Dbse-4jDNj5/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/Dbse-4jDNj5/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands (studio is Amsterdam-based\; collaboration
  terms not specified)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:k3-tanzplan-hamburg-choreography-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: K3 | Tanzplan Hamburg: Choreography Residency 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:K3 in Hamburg is one of the better-funded choreography residenc
 ies in Europe\, and it is aimed squarely at people who want research time 
 rather than a production deadline. Three choreographers are selected for e
 ight months at Kampnagel with a monthly scholarship\, studio access\, a pr
 oduction budget\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support\, plus outr
 each work and a public presentation at the end. Selection runs in two stag
 es. ELIGIBILITY: individual choreographers at an emerging to early mid-car
 eer stage who have already produced independent choreographic work outside
  an educational context. Open internationally\; formal dance training is n
 ot required. Teams and ensembles are not eligible\, only individuals. Appl
 ications close 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: K3 | Tanzpla
 n Hamburg (Kampnagel)\nLocation: Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.\
 nAward: Monthly scholarship (EUR 2\,500 per month in the 2025/26 cycle\; t
 he 2027/28 figure had not been announced at the time of listing)\, plus on
 e-time travel expenses\, visa assistance\, a moderate production budget\, 
 studio access\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support. No applicati
 on fee is stated.\nDuration: Eight months\, August 2027 to April 2028\n\nM
 ore: https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:drift-choreographer-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: DRIFT: Choreographer Open Call 2026-27 (Dance and Researc
 h In The Future Time)
DESCRIPTION:DRIFT (Dance and Research In The Future Time) is a four-year Cr
 eative Europe cooperation between ten European organisations\, led by Thea
 tre de Liege and Maison de la danse. The distinguishing feature is that it
  funds artistic research rather than production: you are expected to arriv
 e with questions\, not a finished piece you already intend to make\, and r
 eviewers read every section of the application through that lens. STRUCTUR
 E: each of the ten partner organisations selects one choreographer from it
 s own country\, forming a cohort of ten. Over the programme you get an Onb
 oarding Lab\, two weeks of residency with your national partner\, three co
 llaborative Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic an
 d curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress showcase at the Biennale d
 e la danse in Lyon\, all backed by a EUR 20\,000 research budget that cove
 rs your fees\, travel\, accommodation and any collaborators. SECOND PHASE:
  after a further evaluation\, five of the ten artists may receive co-produ
 ction support and touring across the DRIFT network. That phase is separate
  from this application and is not guaranteed. ELIGIBILITY: professional ch
 oreographers in contemporary dance or performance\, at a mid-career stage\
 , meaning an established national practice but not yet fully established i
 nternationally. You must be based in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin re
 gion only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portug
 al\, and you apply through the partner organisation representing your coun
 try of residence\, not centrally. Good English and availability for all pr
 ogramme dates are required. The country list is strict\, so check it befor
 e you start. TIMELINE: applications opened 1 July 2026 and close 31 August
  2026\; selection through September\; results 9 October 2026\; programme r
 uns November 2026 to September 2027.\n\nOrganisation: DRIFT\, a four-year 
 Creative Europe cooperation of ten organisations led by Theatre de Liege a
 nd Maison de la danse\nLocation: Activities across Europe: kick-off in Ber
 lin\, Festival Lab in Tunis\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a resi
 dency hosted by your own national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based 
 in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Ita
 ly\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner
  organisation for their country of residence.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 research
  budget per choreographer\, covering artistic fees\, travel\, accommodatio
 n and optional collaborator costs. Also included: an Onboarding Lab\, two 
 weeks of residency with your national DRIFT partner\, three collaborative 
 Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic mentoring and 
 individual curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress presentation at t
 he Biennale de la danse in Lyon. No application fee.\nDuration: November 2
 026 to September 2027. Kick-off in Berlin 30 November to 1 December 2026\;
  Festival Lab in Tunis 18-21 March 2027\; Biennale de la danse showcase in
  Lyon 18-25 September 2027.\n\nMore: https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-ca
 ll?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activities across Europe: kick-off in Berlin\, Festival Lab in Tun
 is\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a residency hosted by your own 
 national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based in Belgium\, France\, Ger
 many (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tu
 nisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner organisation for their c
 ountry of residence.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:scuola-piccola-zattere-international-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260814T140841Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Scuola Piccola Zattere: International Fellowship and Resi
 dency 2027 (Venice)
DESCRIPTION:Scuola Piccola Zattere is an independent non-profit centre for 
 continuous education and research in the expanded field of contemporary ar
 ts\, running exhibitions\, commissions\, fellowships\, workshops\, seminar
 s\, performances and public discussions as one connected research ecosyste
 m rather than separate strands. Two international fellowships are offered.
  The support is unusually solid for a research fellowship: EUR 5\,000 as a
  grant\, EUR 2\,000 as a research budget\, accommodation\, travel and a st
 udy space for three months. SEASON THEME: the 2026-2027 cycle is titled Li
 ving Document\, investigating archives\, regulatory documents\, collective
  rituals\, games\, performative instructions\, and the relationship betwee
 n bodies\, architecture and social organisation. Fellows join that shared 
 inquiry while continuing their own work\, and are expected to keep an acti
 ve presence in the institution and contribute to public events through wor
 kshops\, seminars\, presentations\, screenings or performances. The fellow
 ship overlaps with the run-up to and opening of the 2027 Architecture Bien
 nale\, which is a real advantage if your research benefits from that traff
 ic. ELIGIBILITY: artists\, curators\, researchers\, architects\, choreogra
 phers\, performers and writers of any nationality\, as individuals\, colle
 ctives or duos. Good English is required\, and non-EU applicants need a va
 lid passport for international travel. No age limit\, but the organisers s
 ay it is especially intended for practitioners in the post-academic stage 
 of their career\, typically under 40. You must be prepared to spend the fu
 ll period in Venice\, so this is not a remote or partial-attendance fellow
 ship. WHAT THEY WANT: an active research process that can develop through 
 discussion and experimentation\, tied to the Living Document theme\, not a
  finished stand-alone project proposal. The motivation letter\, portfolio 
 and public programming proposal are read as one integrated application. De
 adline 4 September 2026 at 10:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: Scuola Piccola Zat
 tere\nLocation: Venice\, Italy. Open to any nationality.\nAward: EUR 5\,00
 0 fellowship grant plus a EUR 2\,000 research budget\, accommodation in Ve
 nice\, an individual study space within a shared studio\, and round-trip t
 ravel to Venice. Collectives and duos share a single grant and one accommo
 dation. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months\, in one of two slots:
  early January to early April 2027\, or mid-April to mid-July 2027. Applic
 ants may name one slot or indicate availability for both.\n\nMore: https:/
 /scuolapiccolazattere.com/en/events/internationalfellowship-2-3?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://scuolapiccolazattere.com/en/events/internationalfellowship-2-3?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Venice\, Italy. Open to any nationality.
END:VEVENT
